(borge-bug (id "BORGE-1783398327")
  (title "Spec shift proposal: unify branch tree and AST tree — 'nodes as substrate, named branchable subtrees as the versioning unit' (replaces 'everything is a branch' as the operational model while keeping branches as the versioning model)")
  (status open)
  (created 2026-07-06)
  (filed-by roerick)
  (doc "")
  (* agent note (|Filed by the reviewing agent during a design discussion with roerick (not during an active make/build pass of the building agent). This is a PROPOSAL to be resolved in a future `plan` pass — not an immediate spec edit. Roerick explicitly does not want the spec changed while the other agent is mid-build.

MOTIVATION: two experiments have been done elsewhere — tree-based LLM generation (LLMs creating parent + child nodes) and tree-based editors. The question was whether these combine in Pricklypear. They do, but only if AST nodes become first-class addressable units, which they currently are not.

DIAGNOSIS — there are currently TWO distinct tree structures using two different storage models, two different traversals, and two different operation sets:
  - Branch tree: unit=branch, storage=`branches` row, parent link=`parent_id` column, traversal=recursive CTE in SQL, versioning=fork/merge, addressing=branch_id (UUID), granularity=branch (coarse).
  - AST tree: unit=expression node, storage=JSONB blob inside one function_defs row, parent link=implicit in JSON nesting, traversal=in-memory walk in eval, versioning=rewrite whole body, addressing=NONE (sub-nodes anonymous), granularity=whole function (coarse).

'Everything is a branch' (philosophy point 5, branching section) stops at the function boundary: you can (fork 'main 'experiment) and get a whole branch, but you cannot fork ONE function to try a rewrite, and you cannot fork ONE sub-expression. You can (inspect 'foo) and see the AST, but you cannot address the third argument of the second call in foo's body. The branch tree goes deep (parent chain) but wide (only one level of children — the functions). The AST tree goes deep (nested expressions) but is anonymous (no node IDs) and unversioned (edit = replace the whole JSONB).

PROPOSED SHIFT: 'everything is a tree' as the SUBSTRATE; 'everything is a branch' retained as the VERSIONING MODEL, where a branch is a named subtree. The two trees become one recursive structure.

Concrete shape — give every AST node a UUID and a row:
  nodes (id uuid PK, branch_id uuid, kind text, payload jsonb, parent_id uuid, position int)
A function definition folds into this: a function is kind='lambda', parent_id=null, with child nodes. function_defs becomes a special case (roots only). Then everything uses the same operations:
  - Fork copies a subtree (recursive insert) — same SQL shape as today, made recursive.
  - Override-walk is the recursive CTE already implemented in store.ml (lookup_function, list_function_names, list_routes), applied per-node instead of per-function. A child branch overrides INDIVIDUAL nodes; everything else inherits. Finer than current whole-function override — Alice can override just the body of `greet` while inheriting its lambda signature from main.
  - Merge operates per-node with the same admin-only-to-main policy.
  - Inspect returns a node by UUID or a whole subtree.
  - Edit updates one node's payload; all referrers see it on next resolve.
Plus a `named_subtrees` table mapping names to root node IDs — functions are the default named subtrees, but you can name any subtree. Fork/merge target named subtrees. This keeps 'fork main' / 'merge alice/experiment into main' working unchanged while making fine-grained ops available ('regenerate just this if-branch', 'share this helper subtree across functions').

WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT MOVE FOR LLM + TREE-EDITOR COMBINATION:
  1. Tree-based LLM gen: currently impossible because the LLM must emit a whole function as one JSONB blob. With node addressing, the LLM generates each child node independently — each a separate call, each validated by expr_of_json, each retryable on its own. Smaller units succeed more often; errors localize (regenerate just the failing node, rest of tree preserved); structure is visible between steps (human or agent can inspect partial tree, redirect, LLM generates only the delta). Human-in-the-loop at the node level, not the function level.
  2. Tree-based editor: spec already gestures at 'the htmx tree-editor that edits the AST directly' (lisp-interpreter.reader subsection) but hand-waves it because there's nothing to edit AT — the AST is an anonymous blob. With node IDs, /node/:id renders the node and children as an expandable htmx tree; click a node to edit its payload inline or replace the whole subtree; 'regenerate this node' calls the LLM on just that subtree, persists, parent sees the update. The tree editor and the LLM gen become the SAME UI: every node has expand / edit (human) / regenerate (LLM) affordances. Human edits some nodes, LLM generates others, they interleave on the same tree.
  3. Emergent capability — subtree sharing: once nodes are addressable, two functions can reference the same child node. Edit it once, both update. The LLM can generate a good subtree (a date-parsing helper) and you reference it from five functions without copying. This is `let` bindings made persistent and shared — and also the unit of reuse that makes the branch model sing: (fork) a shared subtree, experiment on your copy, merge back.

NOTE ON THE GIT LESSON (why operations stay coarse even though substrate is fine): Git's substrate is a Merkle tree of blobs, but you branch COMMITS (named subtrees), not individual lines, because fine-grained branching produces combinatorial merge conflicts nobody wants to resolve. The synthesis keeps coarse-unit convenience: nodes are addressable down to expressions, but the OPERATIONS (fork, merge, generate-as-a-unit, edit-as-a-unit) target named subtrees — usually functions, sometimes named sub-expressions, rarely individual nodes. You don't lose 'fork main' convenience; you gain the ability to go fine when you want to.

EVOLUTION PATH (not a big-bang rewrite — the current design is a special case of the proposed one):
  1. Pragmatic v1.5: add UUIDs INSIDE the existing JSONB AST (an `id` field per node), no table change, no eval change, no fork/merge change. Build the /node/:id tree-editor route on top of existing function_defs. Cheapest possible proof that node-addressing and the tree editor work against the real system. (~one route's worth of work.)
  2. Node-level LLM gen: the agent intercept (if/when built) extended to target a node ID ('regenerate subtree at :id'). Small, once the agent intercept exists.
  3. nodes table + resolution pass on load: eval still works on a fully-resolved in-memory AST; hydrate by walking node refs instead of parsing one JSONB blob. Keeps eval pure. (Medium — storage change real, eval barely moves.)
  4. Named subtrees + subtree sharing: new table, new primitive, ref resolution in eval. (Medium.)
  5. Recursive fork/merge over the nodes tree instead of the function_defs table: the real redesign — do this last, once 1-4 have proven the shape.
Recommended first step is #1 (UUIDs in JSONB + tree-editor route) because it de-risks the rest for the cost of one route, and it's the foundation the LLM node-gen plugs into.

SCOPE OF SPEC CHANGES THIS WOULD REQUIRE (to be resolved in plan, not now):
  - philosophy point 5 ('Branches, not namespaces'): refine to 'nodes as substrate, named branchable subtrees as the versioning unit' — branches remain the versioning model, but the substrate is unified.
  - branching.section + subsections fork/merge/schema-authority: generalize from function_defs-row granularity to node granularity; fork becomes recursive subtree copy; merge becomes per-node upsert; override-walk becomes per-node (recursive CTE already implemented, just applied to a different table).
  - lisp-interpreter.ast: the expr ADT stays, but storage representation changes from 'JSONB blob in function_defs.body' to 'rows in nodes table'; eval still operates on a fully-resolved in-memory expr (hydrated from node refs).
  - lisp-interpreter.reader: the 'htmx tree-editor that edits the AST directly' aside becomes a real planned feature with /node/:id addressing, not a hand-wave.
  - storage.schema: add nodes table (or document UUIDs-in-JSONB v1.5 as the interim); function_defs becomes a view/special case.
  - image.htmx-interface: add system pages /node/:id (tree editor) and /subtree/:name (named subtree browser).

RELATED BUT SEPARATE (do not bundle into this issue): the 'agent-mediated REPL as primary interface, htmx as display layer' interface-layer shift is a different proposal also discussed. File separately if pursued.

Filed for roerick's review and a future plan pass. The building agent currently mid-make should NOT act on this — it stays in triage until the next plan cycle.|) *)
